  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/minesweeper/', label: 'Play Minesweeper' }}
>
  <p>
    Minesweeper looks intimidating — a grid full of blank squares, a hidden field of mines — but the
    rules are simple and you can learn them in a couple of minutes. This guide covers the basics from
    the ground up: what the board is, what the numbers mean, how to reveal and flag squares, and the
    handful of deductions that let you clear a board safely. The fastest way to follow along is to
    <a href="/minesweeper/">open a game</a> and read this alongside it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Minesweeper?</h2>
  <p>
    Minesweeper is a single-player logic puzzle. A grid of squares hides a fixed number of mines, and
    your job is to <strong>reveal every safe square without detonating a single mine</strong>. You are
    never asked to find the mines directly — you find the safe squares, and the mines are simply
    everything you leave untouched. It is pure deduction: the board gives you clues, and you work out
    where it is and isn't safe to click.
  </p>

  <h2>The board and the objective</h2>
  <p>
    The board is a rectangular grid of cells. Some unknown subset of those cells contain mines; the
    rest are safe. On CardHearth, Easy is 9×9 with 10 mines, Medium is 16×16 with 40 mines, and
    <a href="/minesweeper-expert/">Expert</a> is the classic 30×16 board with 99 mines. Whatever the
    size, the goal is identical: <strong>clear every cell that is not a mine</strong>. Do that and you
    win — you never need to "click" the mines themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>What the numbers mean</h2>
  <p>
    This is the heart of the game. When you reveal a safe cell, it shows a <strong>number from 1 to
    8</strong>, and that number tells you exactly how many of its eight neighbouring cells (the ones
    touching it horizontally, vertically, and diagonally) contain mines. A 1 means one of those eight
    neighbours is a mine; a 3 means three of them are.
  </p>
  <p>
    A revealed cell that is completely blank is a <strong>0</strong> — none of its neighbours are
    mines. Because a 0 is surrounded entirely by safe cells, the game automatically opens all of those
    neighbours for you, and if any of <em>them</em> are also 0s, it keeps cascading outward. That is
    why a single click in an open area can suddenly clear a large region of the board at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Controls: revealing and flagging</h2>
  <p>
    There are two actions. <strong>Revealing</strong> a cell (a normal click or tap) opens it, showing
    either a number, a blank, or — if you guessed wrong — a mine. <strong>Flagging</strong> a cell
    marks it as a suspected mine so you remember not to click it. On CardHearth you flag with a
    right-click, a long-press on touch screens, or by switching on Flag mode (the ⚑ button or the F
    key).
  </p>
  <p>
    Flags are entirely optional. They do not affect whether you win — they are just a memory aid, and
    a flag does not even have to be correct. The mine counter in the display simply shows the mine
    total minus the flags you have placed, to help you keep track.
  </p>

  <h2>Two things CardHearth does for you</h2>
  <p>
    Two CardHearth features make learning much friendlier than the classic version:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The first click is always safe.</strong> Mines are placed only <em>after</em> your first
      reveal, and the game deliberately keeps them away from the cell you clicked and its neighbours.
      So your opening move can never blow up, and it always opens a starting area to read from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You can undo a misclick.</strong> Hitting a mine no longer permanently ends the game —
      you can take the move back and carry on. Purists can ignore this; beginners can lean on it to
      learn from mistakes without starting over.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Getting started: your first deductions</h2>
  <p>
    After the first click opens an area, work along the edge of the revealed region where the numbers
    sit next to hidden cells. A few simple rules will carry you a long way:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Click in the open.</strong> If you have a large blank area, clicking inside it reveals
      lots of squares at once and gives you more numbers to reason from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Satisfied numbers make the rest safe.</strong> If a number already touches as many flags
      (known mines) as its value, then all of its <em>other</em> hidden neighbours must be safe — you
      can reveal them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forced mines.</strong> If a number's count exactly equals the number of unknown cells
      still touching it, then every one of those unknown cells is a mine. A 1 touching a single hidden
      square means that square is the mine.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Winning and losing</h2>
  <p>
    You <strong>win</strong> the instant every safe cell is revealed — you do not have to flag the
    mines, and CardHearth tracks how many mines remain for you as you go. You <strong>lose</strong> by
    revealing a mine (though, again, undo means a slip need not be the end). Most boards can be solved
    by logic alone; only occasionally will a position leave you with a genuine guess. When that
    happens, and when you want to recognise recurring shapes like the <strong>1-2-1</strong> pattern
    or weigh up the odds of a guess, head to our
    <a href="/guides/minesweeper-strategy/">Minesweeper strategy guide</a>, which covers deduction
    patterns and probability in depth.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    That is everything you need to start playing. <a href="/minesweeper/">Play Minesweeper</a> now and
    practise reading the numbers, then level up with the
    <a href="/guides/minesweeper-strategy/">strategy guide</a> for the patterns and probability that
    crack the harder boards — or tackle the full
    <a href="/minesweeper-expert/">Expert grid</a>. Our <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> covers the
    rest of the CardHearth library too.
  </p>
</Article>
